# The lit flake, as a literate program

This page is lit's own build file, told as a literate program. Everything you
read here tangles back — byte for byte — into the `flake.nix` at the repository
root, and a CI check re-tangles it on every run to prove the two never drift.
It is the first step of lit's self-host path (SPEC.md §13): the Nix half of the
toolchain now presents itself *as* a lit document, and the Nix grammar added
for syntax highlighting (§5.5) exists precisely so this file can be coloured.

A flake is a single attribute set. Ours is one root chunk with a `description`,
a pinned `nixpkgs` input, and an `outputs` function whose `let` block holds two
shared helpers before it exposes the `packages`, `devShells`, and `checks` we
build.
<<flake.nix>>=
{
  description = "lit — a web-first literate programming system built on noweb";

  inputs.nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable";

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs }:
    let
      <<system iteration>>

      <<pinned katex assets>>
    in
    {
      packages = eachSystem (system: pkgs: rec {
        <<the lit package>>
        <<the tangled flake output>>
        default = lit;
      });

      devShells = eachSystem (system: pkgs: {
        <<the dev shell>>
      });

      checks = eachSystem (system: pkgs: {
        <<the checks>>
      });
    };
}
@
## Iterating over systems

Every output is built for the four systems we support. `eachSystem` is the
plumbing: it maps a `system → pkgs → value` function over that list with
`genAttrs`, so each of `packages`, `devShells`, and `checks` is written once and
instantiated per platform.
<<system iteration>>=
systems = [ "x86_64-linux" "aarch64-linux" "x86_64-darwin" "aarch64-darwin" ];
eachSystem = f: nixpkgs.lib.genAttrs systems (system:
  f system nixpkgs.legacyPackages.${system});
@
## Pinning KaTeX

Math renders at weave time, and KaTeX's HTML is version-coupled to its own CSS
and font metrics. The assets we ship must therefore match the exact version the
`katex` crate embeds — nixpkgs' own `katex` is a different release and would
render equations twice. So the version and the fetched asset bundle are pinned
here together, by hash (SPEC.md §6.3).
<<pinned katex assets>>=
# KaTeX renders to HTML that is version-coupled to its own CSS and font
# metrics, so these assets must match the version the `katex` crate
# embeds (0.16.4) — nixpkgs' katex is a different release. See SPEC.md
# §6.3. Bump both together or math silently mislays.
katexVersion = "0.16.4";
katexAssets = pkgs: pkgs.fetchzip {
  url = "https://github.com/KaTeX/KaTeX/releases/download/v${katexVersion}/katex.tar.gz";
  sha256 = "0pgajj8vqfvgws3wnn5bfy4h3w75n4dz8jvjsxcw8s80jnp0md8c";
};
@
## The package

The package is a straightforward `buildRustPackage`, with one wrinkle: noweb's
pipeline stages (`markup`, `nt`, …) live in `lib/noweb`, not `bin`, and the
built `lit` needs both them and the KaTeX assets at runtime. `postFixup` wraps
the binary to put noweb on `PATH` and hand it the assets through
`LIT_KATEX_ASSETS`. Building the package also runs the hermetic cargo tests.
<<the lit package>>=
lit = pkgs.rustPlatform.buildRustPackage {
  pname = "lit";
  version = "0.1.0";
  src = self;
  cargoLock.lockFile = ./Cargo.lock;
  nativeBuildInputs = [ pkgs.makeWrapper ];
  # noweb's pipeline stages (markup, nt, ...) live in lib/noweb,
  # not bin — see SPEC.md §4.2.
  postFixup = ''
    wrapProgram $out/bin/lit \
      --prefix PATH : ${pkgs.noweb}/bin:${pkgs.noweb}/lib/noweb \
      --set LIT_KATEX_ASSETS ${katexAssets pkgs}
  '';
};
@
## The tangled flake, as an output

The dogfood loop as a buildable artifact: `nix build .#flake` re-tangles
`flake.nix.nw` with the freshly built `lit` and emits the `flake.nix` this very
output was built from. It is not circular — building it needs `lit`, which needs
the *committed* `flake.nix` as its permanent bootstrap (SPEC.md §13) — and it
gives `checks.dogfood` one thing to diff the committed file against, rather than
re-tangling a second time.

<<the tangled flake output>>=
# `nix build .#flake` re-tangles flake.nix.nw and emits the flake.nix this
# very output was built from — the dogfood loop as a buildable artifact
# (SPEC.md §13). checks.dogfood diffs the committed file against this.
flake = pkgs.runCommand "flake.nix" { } ''
  cd ${self}
  ${lit}/bin/lit tangle flake.nix.nw --out $TMPDIR
  cp $TMPDIR/flake.nix $out
'';
@
## The dev shell

The dev shell carries the Rust toolchain and noweb, and reproduces the same two
environment tweaks the wrapper bakes into the package so that `cargo run` inside
`nix develop` behaves exactly like the installed binary.
<<the dev shell>>=
default = pkgs.mkShell {
  packages = with pkgs; [ cargo rustc rustfmt clippy rust-analyzer noweb ];
  shellHook = ''
    export PATH="${pkgs.noweb}/lib/noweb:$PATH"
    export LIT_KATEX_ASSETS="${katexAssets pkgs}"
  '';
};
@
## The checks

`nix flake check` is lit's whole test matrix. Each check is an independent
